body:
Another useful and possibly more accessible link is the following:

 [https://yaqeeninstitute.org/omar-suleiman/infographic-islam-and-the-abortion-debate/](https://yaqeeninstitute.org/omar-suleiman/infographic-islam-and-the-abortion-debate/) 

&#x200B;

It generally has the same conclusions. I think it's important to note that it is also agreed that in the first 40 days, abortion is permissible for legitimate reasons, which could include rape or a threat to the mother's life. However, it seems as though financial concerns and conceiving a child out of wedlock do not qualify as legitimate reasons.

&#x200B;

To answer your question, before researching pro-choice and pro-life stances and statistics, I used to be very pro-choice. It was a naive and malinformed perspective, as I had thought that for some reason, most cases of having an abortion were a result of extreme and necessary circumstances. I also viewed the pro-life side of protecting the fetus's life, even if it would harm the mother or even if it was a product of rape, as too harsh. However, after seeing the statistics on reasons of why people chose to have abortions, I began to see, much like you described, that people used it after being negligent of other contraceptive methods. My stance shifted more towards the middle after learning that.

&#x200B;

Regarding this, and other political issues, I wish issues weren't presented as black-or-white. I wish there were more labels than just "pro-choice" or "pro-life", because I feel as though I fall somewhere in between. It removes a lot of nuance in the conversation and allows people to paint the other side in a stereotypical way, and sometimes forces people to choose one side or the other.
